{"id": "127012", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=127012", "title": "Fulton", "text": "Fulton is the name of multiple cities, some of which are named for Robert Fulton (1765\u20131815) as inventor of the first commercially successful steam-powered ship.\nUnited States of America.\nand:\nThere is also a historically-designated Fulton Opera House in downtown Lancaster (Pennsylvania)."}
{"id": "127024", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=127024", "title": "Fort Frances", "text": "Fort Frances is a town of 7,700 people (2016) in Northern Ontario. Fort Frances is a popular fishing destination. It hosts the annual Fort Frances Canadian Bass Championship.\nUnderstand.\nFort Frances sits on the international border with the United States where Rainy Lake narrows to become Rainy River. It is connected to International Falls, Minnesota, by the Fort Frances\u2013International Falls International Bridge. The Fort Frances Mill was the main employer in the town until it closed in January 2014.\nHistory.\nFort Frances was the first European settlement west of Lake Superior and was established by French Canadian Pierre Gaultier de Varennes, sieur de La V\u00e9rendrye, first commander of the western district. In 1731 he built Fort Saint Pierre near this spot as support for the fur trade with native peoples. In 1732 his expedition built Fort Saint Charles on Magnuson Island on the west side of Lake of the Woods. After some time, Fort St. Pierre fell out of use.\nIn 1817, following the War of 1812 and redefinition of borders between Canada and the United States, the Hudson's Bay Company (HBC) built a fort here. In 1830 HBC Chief Factor John Dugald Cameron named the fur trading post after Frances Ramsay Simpson, the 18-year-old daughter of a London merchant, who had married earlier that year in London, George Simpson, Governor of the Hudson's Bay Company, who would visit the fort many times.\nClimate.\nIn June, July and August, daytime temperatures range from 25-30 \u00b0C (77-86 \u00b0F), though nights may be cool, particularly in the north. In autumn, foliage takes on fall colours towards the end of September and reaches its height in October. Medium to heavy-weight clothing is necessary, especially in November when the weather is cold and frosty. Snowfall is usually experienced through December, January and February. The snow begins to recede in March before totally disappearing in April. This is followed by warm temperatures and spring flowers in May.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nFrom elsewhere in Ontario.\nOntario Highways 11 and 71 (both part of the Trans-Canada Highway) run through town. It is 435 km from Thunder Bay and 215 km from Kenora.\nFrom the United States.\nThe Fort Frances-International Falls Bridge (the start/end point of Ontario Highway 71) connects Fort Frances with International Falls, Minnesota, USA. U.S. Highways 53 and 71 lead up to the bridge on the U.S. side. \nTolls are collected northbound only (entering Canada); no tolls are collected southbound. As of August 2024, the toll for passenger vehicles is US$9 / C$11.\nDo.\nLicences are required for fishing or hunting in Ontario and may be purchased from sports stores, tourist outfitters, hunting and fishing lodges and the Ministry of Natural Resources."}
{"id": "127039", "revid": "160739", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=127039", "title": "Orust", "text": "Just off the Bohusl\u00e4n coast, Orust is Sweden's fourth largest island. Only Gotland and \u00d6land are larger, as well as S\u00f6dert\u00f6rn, which appears as a peninsula more than an island. Orust Municipality, \"Orusts kommun\", also contains some minor islands.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nThe islands Orust and Tj\u00f6rn are connected to the mainland by two bridges, one in the north and one in the south, and there is also a bridge between the islands. Road 160 uses these bridges. The bridge in south is particularly remarkable, and there are rest areas nearby that are all recommended to visit.\nBy bus.\nV\u00e4sttrafik organises buses to Orust from Uddevalla and Stenungsund."}
{"id": "127046", "revid": "100394", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=127046", "title": "Rail and bus travel in Sweden", "text": "Sweden has an extensive railway network. Long-distance lines are operated by the government-owned company SJ AB, or by private enterprises. Regional public transportation, both by rail and bus, is provided by the counties, \"l\u00e4n\", or associations of those. There are also some commercial bus services.\nPlanning.\nThe transport carriers in Sweden operate a ticket platform called Resplus for multiple-leg travel. See Resrobot for an interactive journey planner.\nUsing Google Maps to find train connections to build an itinerary can work very well if you are doing it weeks or months ahead of time. If you are close to the day of travel, or you are on the ground, you may find that you have more data than Google and their data can sometimes be wrong. Or Google Maps may not offer you the best connection points when there are multiple stations at which you can make the connection. Double check your plan with staff at a station, if you are booking within a few days of travel. \nNational rail carriers.\nWhile rail travel is deregulated in Sweden, national carrier SJ remains the market leader. While rail speed is far behind countries such as France and Spain, rail is a good option for intercity travel.\nMany routes connect to Copenhagen and Oslo, and some to Trondheim, Narvik and Berlin. Railways in Finland have a broader track gauge, and are therefore not connected to the Swedish rail network. Furthermore, most of the area where Sweden and Finland share a land border is extremely sparsely populated. There is a double gauge railway at the border in Haparanda/Tornio but no passenger trains use it.\nTickets can be bought either through Resplus or directly from the specific carrier, and are normally reserved for a specific train departure. Regional public transport tickets might be used for any regional public train departure within a given time, but those tickets are normally not valid on national rail carrier trains.\nSJ.\nTo buy a railway ticket from SJ, or to obtain information, call \u260f or check the SJ website. As of summer 2009, the cheapest SJ tickets are released exactly 90 days before departure, so time your online ticket purchases carefully if your itinerary is set and don't buy tickets earlier than 90 days before your trip. Because point-to-point tickets are quite expensive, for more train journeys in Sweden European rail passes might be useful. If you are travelling to small towns in Sweden and you need to journey long distances from there, do not leave your tickets unbooked until the last minute. The busy trains that would offer you fast travel and better connections can get sold out during the summer, sometimes a few days in advance.\nUnlike on most European trains, bicycles are not allowed on any SJ trains, except for foldable bicycles, which count as regular luggage. \nThere are sleeper train services between Stockholm and Malm\u00f6 as well as between Gothenburg, Stockholm and the northern parts of Norrland. The night trains can be delayed several hours in bad winter weather.\nVR.\nFinnish national rail operator VR has a fast train between Stockholm and Gothenburg (Formerly MTR Express), with seasonal service to Halland and Sk\u00e5ne. Tickets are sold only on https://www.vrresa.se (not from machines or Resplus). They serve food on board.\nSn\u00e4llt\u00e5get.\nSn\u00e4llt\u00e5get (brand name operated by Transdev, formerly Veolia) operates some rail lines; from Malm\u00f6 to Berlin, Stockholm and \u00c5re (during ski season, from December to April).\nRegional public transportation.\nRegional public transport can include commuter rail, tram, metro (in Stockholm), bus or boat (in the Swedish archipelagos), and is usually provided by the counties (\"l\u00e4n\"), under the brand name of the county owned traffic company.\nTickets are normally sold by the county owned traffic company. Ticket rules vary substantially. For regional trains it is normally possible to buy a ticket through SJ (see below) even if it is a county operated train. Depending on how small a town is, there may not be staff at the train station to sell you tickets. Look for an automated machine in this case, which will walk you through the booking process. They are usually found in enclosed glass buildings that can often be mistaken for a bus stop (but they will be on the platform).\nUnlike on SJ trains, it is common on regional trains for the announcements to be made in Swedish alone. Rail carriages do have LED displays would show what station is next and other information. Note what your station names should sound like and listen to the Swedish announcements to hear whether they might concern you. Ask your fellow passengers or a passing-by train conductor to find out more. It is rare to find a Swede who doesn't speak English at some level.\nThere should be more information in the region articles.\nFor instance, when travelling regionally in the province of Scania (\"Sk\u00e5ne\" in Swedish), one should refer to Sk\u00e5netrafiken.\nTickets.\nCity and county buses are part of the county's public transit system. Check out the local arrangements for how to obtain tickets. In many Swedish cities it is not possible to buy tickets for the city buses at the bus. In this case neither cash nor bank or credit cards are accepted. Instead you need an electronic bus card, a special card for each region, that sometimes also has to be filled with a minimum amount of money, typically 100\u00a0kr. This bus card can sometimes be obtained only at dedicated ticket offices, not at the bus, but can often be filled with money for travel at local shops or refill machines that are found at public places. This does not apply to countryside and long distance buses, where you normally can buy tickets from the driver.\nFor trams, there are (Gothenburg/Norrk\u00f6ping) normally machines onboard or (Stockholm) salesperson onboard. For Stockholm metro and commuter trains, tickets are sold at the station entrances.\nIn general, tourists are advised to buy one-day or multi-day passes, which cover access to local public traffic.\nUrban rail.\nStockholm has a metro line known for its art; see Stockholm County#Stockholm Metro, as well as light rail lines in some suburbs.\nGothenburg and Norrk\u00f6ping are known for their iconic trams.\nCommercial buses.\nFlixbus and Nettbuss run a number of bus lines in the southern third of the country (G\u00f6taland and Svealand). They tend to cost less than going by train, if you can't take advantage of SJ's youth discounts. Y-buss, H\u00e4rjedalingen and Tapanis Buss operate between Stockholm and Norrland.\nFlixbus also operates from Stockholm and Gothenburg to Oslo.\nAt the county level, buses are a good method for travelling short distances from town to town, as they are more frequent and cheaper than trains. It is best to check with the local transportation authority for routes and schedules.\nFlygbussarna.\nFlygbussarna, \"Airport Coaches\", are a private enterprise specialising in transfer between airports (or ferry terminals) and city centres."}
