{"id": "128656", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=128656", "title": "Ploie\u0219ti", "text": "Ploie\u0219ti (Pronounced: \"Plo-yesht\") is the closest city to Bucharest in Romania (2021 population: 180,540). The city has an attractive core with a collection of 19th-century mansions, unique museums, and excellent rail connections to the rest of the country, making it a convenient base or jumping-off point while exploring Romania.\nKnown as the birthplace of the modern oil refining industry, it was home to the world\u2019s first large-scale oil refinery in 1856 and remained a major refining hub throughout the 20th century. While not a touristy city, Ploie\u0219ti draws international visitors engaged with the various local industries and businesses and foreign students at the Oil and Gas University (UPG).\nUnderstand.\nPloie\u0219ti traces its formal founding to 1596, when Michael the Brave, Prince of Wallachia, is said to have established it as a market town, though the area had been inhabited for centuries before. Its rise coincided with the decline of nearby T\u00e2rg\u0219or, once one of the most important cities in Wallachia, now a small village barely 13 km away from Ploiesti. As T\u00e2rg\u0219or\u2019s trade and influence faded in the late 16th and 17th centuries, merchants and craftsmen gravitated to the growing market in Ploie\u0219ti, drawn by its position on the crossroads linking Bucharest with Moldavia and Transylvania.\nBy the late 19th century, it had grown into one of Romania\u2019s key commercial hubs, thanks to its industries, thriving vineyards on the hills to the east, and strategic position. During this time, merchants from across the country gathered here, and the city gained a reputation for its lively spirited nightlife. At its height, just after the First World War, there was said to be a pub for every 200 residents in Ploie\u0219ti.\nDuring the Second World War, Romania was the largest producer of oil in Europe, with local refineries from Ploie\u0219ti supplying nearly a third of Nazi Germany's oil needs. This led to repeated bombing raids, the most famous being the 1943 U.S. bombing known as \"Operation Tidal Wave\".\nToday, Ploie\u0219ti remains an industrial centre with four active oil refineries, though its proximity to Bucharest has drawn much of the younger residents to the capital or abroad in search of new opportunities.\nLocal information.\nPloie\u0219ti city[dead link] website\nPloie\u0219ti City App Android / iOS\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nPloie\u015fti is served by the same airport as Bucharest, the Henri Coand\u0103 International Airport that is 45 km away. There is a rail line connecting the airport with the main train station of Bucharest where you can easily hop on a train to Ploie\u0219ti at any time before 22:30 (\u20ac3-5). Taxis/Uber/Bolt can also make the journey from the airport to Ploie\u0219ti but at a significantly higher cost (\u20ac25 to \u20ac40).\nBy train.\nPloie\u015fti is the second most important railway hub in Romania and is linked by numerous trains with every major city in the country (there are more than 60 trains daily connecting Bucharest) through its train stations.\nTrains take between 35 and 70 minutes to cover the distance between Bucharest and Ploie\u015fti, depending on the type of train. The train is the fastest way to get to Ploie\u015fti, both from Bucharest and Bra\u0219ov.\nTransfer between Gara de Sud and Gara de Vest.\nA dozen or so trains a day connect the two stations, running regularly. The train journey takes 6 minutes. Any train in Gara de Sud with a destination in Transylvania will stop in Gara de Vest and any train in Gara de Vest with a destination in Moldavia or M\u0103neciu will stop in Gara de Sud.\nOtherwise, bus line 2 connects the two train stations within 20 minutes. Taxis take 10-15 minutes at a cost of roughly \u20ac2.\nBy bus.\nPloie\u015fti is home to two official bus stations and one minibus station. Using the bus in the region is not recommended (except perhaps going to T\u00e2rgovi\u015fte, taking a flixbus, or to local places lacking train connections). Service schedules can be a mystery, even to locals. Online information may or may not be out of date. To make matters more complicated, a host of other spots are used as ad-hoc picking up and dropping off locations for various transport services, making navigating the system very difficult for travelers.\nBy car.\nPloie\u015fti is very well connected with other cities in Romania, being very close to Bucharest and placed almost midway between the capital and Bra\u0219ov. From Bucharest, in can be reached either through the old DN1 national road or the A3 motorway. The drive from the airport takes around 40 minutes. From Bucharest, the drive can take anywhere between 50 minutes and two hours, depending on congestion levels and how far in Bucharest you start.\nThere are ample parking locations in the city centre, though best is to use one of the two multi-story car parks. The price for parking is around 2.5 lei per hour.\nGet around.\nOn foot.\nPloie\u0219ti is quite compact and easy to explore on foot, especially in the central area where most attractions are.\nBy public transport.\nThe city\u2019s public transport network, operated by Transport C\u0103l\u0103tori Express Ploie\u0219ti (TCE), covers all districts with 38 bus routes, 4 trolleybus lines, and 2 tram lines. Public transport navigation is available on Google Maps, though be aware that timetable timings are often unreliable and some routes run less frequently than expected.\nSingle fares cost 2.5 lei and can be paid directly with a contactless bank card on board. Paper tickets are also available at kiosks near major stops.\nBy taxi.\nTaxis and ride-hailing (Uber, Bolt) are widely available at all hours, and a trip across the city rarely costs more than 25 lei. While most drivers are honest, a few may try to overcharge by claiming the meter is broken or taking a longer route if they suspect you\u2019re a visitor. To avoid surprises, confirm that the meter is running before starting your journey or use a ride-hailing app where the fare is set in advance.\nSee.\nPloie\u0219ti\u2019s historic architecture suffered heavily during the 1943 U.S. bombing raids and in the following decades under Communist rule, which replaced much of the old city with the stark concrete brutalist buildings typical of southern Romania. Quite a bit of the surviving old buildings lay abandoned in a poor state or have been improperly refurbished, not taking the historical design into account. Still, a number of gems remain that reveal the city\u2019s layered past.\nDo.\nCultural events.\nMany events taking place in Ploie\u0219ti can be found on the official Ploie\u0219ti City App.\nBuy.\nPloiesti is hardly a city known for shopping. Being so close to Bucharest, shopping options are quite limited and basic as they mostly cater to the daily needs of the local population.\nEat.\nMany restaurants are dotted around the city center as well as the northern strip right before exiting the city towards Ploiesti Shopping City. There is variety in food choices, with quite a few sushi, Greek, Italian, and Turkish places, alongside the typical Romanian.\nDrink.\nBars and pubs.\nThere are a few good bars and beer gardens in the city. Nightlife is limited to bars rather than clubs.\nCafes.\nThere are plenty of cafes in the city. \nConnect.\nThere is free Wi-Fi across most of the city center and inside most, if not all, bars and restaurants. Internet speeds are among the best and cheapest in the whole world (500-1000 Mbps is the typical broadband speed)."}
{"id": "128660", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=128660", "title": "Ningde", "text": "Ningde (\u5b81\u5fb7) is a coastal city in the eastern part of Fujian Province, known for its rich natural resources and unique maritime culture. The city boasts beautiful beaches, mountains, islands, and abundant ecological landscapes, making it an ideal destination for tourism and vacation. Ningde is also an important base for China's new energy industry, with rapid development and distinctive historical and cultural charm.\nUnderstand.\nThe city of Ningde is near the southern border of the prefecture. There is some spectacular scenery in Ningde prefecture, but mainly well outside of Ningde city itself.\nHistory.\nNingde has a long history, dating back to the Qin and Han dynasties, and has undergone numerous political and cultural changes over time. As an important port on the Maritime Silk Road, Ningde has a rich maritime culture and trade background, and still preserves a wealth of traditional and cultural heritage today.\nGeography.\nNingde City is located in the eastern part of Fujian Province and administers 1 district (Jiao District) and 7 counties (Fu'an, Shouning, Xiapu, Zhouning, Pingnan, Gutian, and Fuding). The region is rich in natural resources, including mountains, coastlines, and ecological attractions, making it ideal for tourism and leisure. Ningde's economy is developing rapidly, particularly in the fields of new energy, fishing, agriculture, and tourism. Each district and county has its unique local features, such as seafood and tea in Fu'an, coastal scenery and photography resources in Xiapu, and natural landscapes and traditional cuisine in Shouning.\nGet in.\nOther stations in Ningde prefecture that may be useful for accessing local attractions are:\nThere is also a good highway along the coast which connects Fuzhou, Ningde, Xiapu and Wenzhou, just to the north in Zhejiang province. From Wenzhou, there are good road connections all over East China.\nThis region has no civilian airports, though there is a large military one. There are buses direct from Ningde to Fuzhou airport and Wenzhou is nearby to the north; both have good connections all over China and a few international flights. The nearest airports with a good range of international flights are Xiamen to the south or Hangzhou and Shanghai to the north; all are easily reached via the fast trains."}
{"id": "128661", "revid": "898440", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=128661", "title": "Xiapu", "text": "Xiapu is located in northeastern Fujian Province, China, is a county under the jurisdiction of Ningde City, known as \"sea wonderland\". Known for its unique beach landscape and rich marine resources, it is a paradise for photographers, especially for tidal change, fishermen's labor scenes and beautiful sunrise and sunset views. Xiapu also has a long history and rich seafood resources, is a good place for tourism, leisure and experience fishing culture.\nUnderstand.\nIt is famous for its natural landscape, mudflats, marine culture and fishermen's lifestyle, especially its mudflats ecosystem and rich marine resources. Xia Pu is one of the important fishing areas in China, and fishing, seaweed and laver cultivation are important parts of the local economy. The tidal changes in Kasumigaseki and the beautiful scenery of the coastline also make it a popular destination for photographers and tourists both at home and abroad. In addition, Xia Pu has a deep cultural heritage and has many traditional folk arts and historical sites.\nHistory.\nXia Pu has a long history, as early as the Eastern Han Dynasty, humans settled here. It is an important coastal city in eastern Fujian and was once one of the important ports of the ancient Maritime Silk Road. With the development of maritime transportation, Xia Pu has gradually become an important base for fishing, salt industry and foreign trade. In modern times, Xia Pu has attracted a large number of tourists with its unique natural scenery and fishermen's culture, becoming one of the well-known tourist destinations in Fujian Province.\nClimate.\nThe Xiapu area belongs to the mid-subtropical monsoon humid climate zone, with an average annual temperature of 16 \u2103-19 \u2103, rainy in spring, typhoons in summer, warm in winter and cool in summer, frost and snow are rare. Affected by the ocean climate, the monsoon has obvious characteristics. Disastrous weather is dominated by typhoons and heavy rains, and serious typhoons occur 3 times a year on average.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nXiapu does not have its own airport, the nearest airport is Fuzhou Changle International Airport in Fuzhou City, Fujian, about 150 km from Xiapu.\nBy car.\nSelf-driving is another convenient way to get into Xiapu.\nFrom Fuzhou: follow Shenhai Expressway (G15) southbound, turn Fu Ning Expressway to Xiapu, the whole journey is about 2 hours.\nFrom Xiamen: along the Shenhai Expressway northbound, the whole journey is about 5 hours.\nFrom Wenzhou: along the Shenhai Expressway southbound, the whole journey is about 3 hours.\nBy boat.\nXia Pu is a coastal county, but there are no regular passenger ships (as of Dec 2024), only fishing boats or private speedboat services in the surrounding area, suitable for short sea tours.\nGet around.\nBy bus\nLocal buses are the most common and economical way to travel within Xiapu. They connect the county center with nearby towns, villages, and scenic areas, including the famous mudflat photography spots. Fares typically range from \u00a52 to \u00a510 depending on the distance.\nBy taxi\nTaxis are widely available in Xiapu. The starting fare is around \u00a58, and longer trips to outlying areas are negotiable. For trips to popular tourist spots, consider hiring a taxi for half a day or a full day for more convenience.\nBy motorcycle taxi\nMotorcycle taxis are a quick and inexpensive way to navigate the town. Fares start at \u00a55 but may vary based on the distance and negotiation. These are more suitable for short trips within the county center.\nBy bicycle\nRenting a bicycle is a great way to explore Xiapu's rural landscapes and coastline. Daily rental prices range from \u00a520 to \u00a550. \nBy private car or rideshare\nPrivate car hire or ridesharing apps like Didi are ideal for reaching remote scenic areas. Prices depend on the distance and duration of use, with daily rentals starting at \u00a5300.\nOn foot\nThe county center of Xiapu is compact and walkable. Many shops, restaurants, and services are within a short walking distance from hotels and the bus station. For a more immersive experience, walking around villages or coastal areas is highly recommended.\nEat.\nXiapu Seafood Street.\nEntrance Address: No.88 Haibin Road, Songgang Street\nOpening Hours: 11:00 - 23:00\nPrice: Per capita consumption of about \u00a5100, the specific price depends on the order. \nKnown for its fresh seafood, the Xiapu Seafood Bar offers a variety of local specialties and is loved by tourists and local residents.\nCape Restaurant.\nAddress: No. 5, Haijing Road, Haijiao Village, Shajiang Town\nOpening Hours: 10:00 - 22:00\nPrice: Per capita consumption of about 80 yuan, the specific price depends on the order. \nCape Restaurant is known for its unique view of the sea and delicious seafood dishes, making it an ideal place to enjoy food and view.\nEast Hill Restaurant.\nAddress: No. 200 Dongshan Road\nOpening Hours: Daily 11:00 - 22:00\nPrice: Per capita consumption of about \u00a5120, the specific price depends on the order. \nDongshan Restaurant is famous for its exquisite Fujian cuisine and high-quality service, suitable for business dinners and family dinners.\nDrink.\nCoffee shop.\nAddress: No. 12, Songgang Street Culture Road, \nOpening hours: Daily 09:00 - 22:00\nPrice: Average price of coffee \u00a525-40, average price of dessert \u00a515-30\nFeatures: Warm and comfortable environment, providing boutique coffee, handmade desserts, is suitable for relaxation or office.\nFishing port bar.\nAddress: Building 2, Fisherman's Wharf, Binhai Road\nOpening hours: Daily 18:00 - 02:00\nPrice: Average price of cocktail \u00a550-80, average price of beer \u00a530-50\nFeatures\uff1aA warm atmosphere with a wide selection of alcoholic beverages and live music, it is the first choice for nightlife enthusiasts.\nCoffee shop.\nAddress: No. 20, Baobao Village\nOpening hours: Daily 08:00 - 20:00\nPrice: Average price of tea \u00a520-50, average price of traditional snacks \u00a510-20 \nFeatures: Provide authentic Fujian tea culture experience, antique environment, suitable for small gatherings with friends or tea relaxation."}
{"id": "128662", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=128662", "title": "Williamson (New York)", "text": "Williamson is a rural community in the Finger Lakes Apple Country region of New York State.\nThe town is located in Wayne County, NY and named after the land agent of the Pulteney Purchase, Charles Williamson.\nThe hamlet of Pultneyville is on Lake Ontario, in the northern portion of the town, and the hamlet of East Williamson is in the eastern part. It is bordered by Ontario to the west, Sodus to the east, and Marion to the south."}
{"id": "128663", "revid": "2183461", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=128663", "title": "Milton (New Zealand)", "text": "The little town of Milton is north of Balclutha and about 50km south of Dunedin in Otago, New Zealand.\nUnderstand.\nThe population is only about 2,000 and some of Milton's few streets are named for British writers such as Burns, Chaucer, Johnson, Scott, Shakespeare and Spencer. For most travellers, Milton is just a toilet stop on SH1 which passes right through its centre.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nMilton is served by Intercity coaches as a stop on Dunedin - Queenstown, Christchurch - Te Anau and Dunedin - Invercargill routes."}
